  a username and a password and nothing else. A user ID is normally something login [3mestablishes[0m (server looks up the
  credentials and tells you who that was), not something the client hands in. A client-supplied [38;5;153muserId[39m is also the
  classic spoofing hole: whoever calls [38;5;153mlogin[39m gets to assert who they are, unverified. So "add a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter" as
  literally written has no value to put in it.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether "track who logged in" is satisfied by plumbing an identifier you already have from somewhere, or
  whether the identifier has to be produced and recorded — those diverge in scope and in what the interface ends up
  looking like.

  - [1mReturn the ID instead of accepting it[0m — [38;5;153mlogin[39m resolves the user and returns [38;5;153m{ success, userId }[39m, caller logs it.
    Buys correct, unspoofable attribution; costs nothing extra now; keeps the signature honest. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mAccept a caller-supplied [38;5;153muserId[0m — literally what you asked. Buys a one-line change; costs a parameter no current
    caller can fill, and bakes client-asserted identity into the interface. Expensive to walk back once callers depend
    on it.
  - [1mBuild the tracking[0m — a logging/analytics path that records login events. This is a new subsystem; repo has none.
    Architectural, gets the full treatment.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the second option, which is what you literally asked for, is the one I'd push back on — not because
  the change is hard, but because there's no caller that can supply the value, so it would land dead. If you have a
  context I'm not seeing (a session ID, a tenant ID, an SSO subject already on the page), tell me and it becomes bounded
  and I'll do it as asked.
